L431: All experiments were conducted using standard CPUs with limited cores, validating that our approach is suitable for on-device learning scenarios where GPU acceleration may not be available. To run these experiments (both with and without SPR), we used 4 CPU cores with 4GB RAM. The networks and the training code was written in JAX.
L432: As shown in Table cite175†6 , the streaming DQN and Stream Q($\lambda$) agents completed 40M frames of training in roughly 20 to 35 hours, while the QRC($\lambda$) variants took longer, taking about 35 to 50 hours. In the original SPR codebase, 400k frames experiments take from 2 to 6 hours to complete depending on the algorithm. We also list the total trainable parameter size for each algorithm.
L433: ### A.10 SPR and Orthogonal Updates Algorithm
L434: 
L435: Algorithm 1 Streaming Self-Predictive Representations with Orthogonal Updates
L436: 
L437: 1:  Input: Stream of transitions
L438: 
L439: 2:  Parameters: $\theta$ (online enc), $\theta^{\prime}$ (target enc), $\psi$ (transition), $\phi$ (online proj), $\phi^{\prime}$ (target proj), $\omega$ (predictor), $\xi$ (Q-head)
L440: 
L441: 3:  Config: Flags orth, orth^{2}
L442: 
L443: 4:  Denote prediction horizon as $K$
L444: 5:  Initialize momentum history $m\leftarrow 0$ for all SPR modules $\eta\in\{\theta,\psi,\phi,\omega\}$
L445: 
L446: 6:
L447: 
L448: 7:  for step $t$ in training do
L449: 
L450: 8:   Collect experience $(s_{t},a_{t},r_{t},s_{t+1})$
L451: 
L452: 9:   Store transition in temporary buffer
L453: 
L454: 10:   if buffer size $<K$ then
L455: 
L456: 11:    continue
L457: 
L458: 12:   end if
L459: 
L460: 13:   {SPR Phase: Recompute latents from past states}
L461: 
L462: 14:   Retrieve trajectory $s_{t-K+1},\dots,s_{t+1}$ and actions $a_{t-K+1},\dots,a_{t}$
L463: 
L464: 15:   if augmentation then
L465: 16:    $s\leftarrow\text{augment}(s)$ for all $s$ in trajectory
L466: 
L467: 17:   end if
L468: 
L469: 18:   $z\leftarrow f_{\theta}(s_{t-K+1})$ {Initial online latent}
L470: 
L471: 19:   Initialize SPR loss $l_{SPR}\leftarrow 0$
L472: 
L473: 20:   for $k=0$ to $K-1$ do
L474: 
L475: 21:    $\hat{z}_{next}\leftarrow D_{\psi}(z,a_{t-K+1+k})$ {Transition model}
L476: 
L477: 22:    $\tilde{z}_{next}\leftarrow f_{\theta^{\prime}}(s_{t-K+1+k+1})$ {Target representations}
L478: 
L479: 23:    $\hat{y}\leftarrow q_{\omega}(P_{\phi}(\hat{z}_{next}))$ {Online projection & prediction}
L480: 24:    $\tilde{y}\leftarrow P_{\phi^{\prime}}(\tilde{z}_{next})$ {Target projection}
L481: 
L482: 25:    $l_{SPR}\leftarrow l_{SPR}-\left(\frac{\hat{y}}{\|\hat{y}\|_{2}}\cdot\frac{\texttt{SG}[\tilde{y}]}{\|\texttt{SG}[\tilde{y}]\|_{2}}\right)$ {Cosine similarity}
L483: 
L484: 26:    $z\leftarrow\hat{z}_{next}$
L485: 
L486: 27:   end for
L487: 
L488: 28:   $\delta_{SPR}\leftarrow\nabla_{\theta,\psi,\phi,\omega}(\lambda_{SPR}l_{SPR})$ {Compute SPR gradients}
L489: 
L490: 29:   {Decorrelating Updates (Sec. cite18†4.2 )}
L491: 
L492: 30:   if orth then
L493: 31:    for each module param $\eta\in\{\theta,\psi,\phi,\omega\}$ do
L494: 
L495: 32:     $\delta_{\eta}\leftarrow\delta_{\eta}-\text{proj}_{m_{\eta}}(\delta_{\eta})$ {Orthogonalize current grad against history}
L496: 
L497: 33:     $m_{\eta}\leftarrow\beta m_{\eta}+(1-\beta)\delta_{\eta}$ {Update history}
L498: 
L499: 34:    end for
L500: 
L501: 35:   end if
L502: 
L503: 36:   {RL Phase & Conflict Resolution (Sec. cite19†4.3 )}
L504: 
L505: 37:   $u^{RL}\leftarrow\text{ObGDVector}(s_{t},a_{t},r_{t},s_{t+1};\theta,\xi)$ {Calculates updates for shared enc and Q-head}
L506: 38:   if orth^{2} and using ObGD then
L507: 
L508: 39:    for shared params $\theta$ do
L509: 
L510: 40:     $\delta_{\theta}\leftarrow\delta_{\theta}-\text{proj}_{u^{RL}_{\theta}}(\delta_{\theta})$ {Project SPR grad away from RL update}
L511: 
L512: 41:    end for
L513: 
L514: 42:   end if
L515: 
L516: 43:   {Apply combined updates}
L517: 
L518: 44:   $\theta\leftarrow\theta+u^{RL}_{\theta}-\alpha\delta_{\theta}$ {Update shared encoder}
L519: 
L520: 45:   $\xi\leftarrow\xi+u^{RL}_{\xi}$ {Update Q-head (RL only)}
L521: 46:   $\psi,\phi,\omega\leftarrow\text{optimizer}(\psi,\phi,\omega;-\alpha\delta_{SPR})$ {Update SPR modules}
L522: 
L523: 47:
L524: 
L525: 48:   $\theta^{\prime}\leftarrow(1-\tau)\theta+\tau\theta^{\prime}$ {Update target encoder (EMA)}
L526: 
L527: 49:   $\phi^{\prime}\leftarrow(1-\tau)\phi+\tau\phi^{\prime}$ {Update target projection (EMA)}
L528: 
L529: 50:  end for
L530: ## Appendix B Additional Results
L531: Figure 5: The aggregate metrics with a 95% confidence interval for the algorithms across different domains: Atari (TOP), MinAtar (MIDDLE) and Octax (BOTTOM). Here IQM stands for interquartile mean. Each experiment was run for five random seeds. We see that adding SPR increases aggregate performance for both streaming DQN and QRC($\lambda$) across all metrics. The addition of orthogonal updates also helps SPR work better in the streaming setting.
L532: SPR fails to work with Stream Q($\lambda$) due to gradient conflicts. However after orthogonalizing SPR gradients with respect to ObGD gradients as described in Sec. cite19†4.3 , we see a marked improvement in SPR performance, with a higher median and IQM than Stream Q($\lambda$).
L533: Figure 6: The performance profiles of the different algorithms across the Atari, MinAtar and Octax games, displaying their probability of achieving a certain score. We see that for QRC($\lambda$) and DQN adding SPR loss leads to an increase in performance across scores. For Stream Q($\lambda$), the orth^{2} method turns out to be better or on par with the base algorithm
